1
00:00:00,182 --> 00:00:04,361
There are only four kinds of wrong
answers on SAT notes questions.

2
00:00:04,790 --> 00:00:07,040
True, but off the goal.

3
00:00:07,254 --> 00:00:10,091
The right kind of answer
with the wrong detail.

4
00:00:10,111 --> 00:00:12,039
One side of a comparison only.

5
00:00:12,254 --> 00:00:14,448
And the fourth one looks completely right,

6
00:00:14,468 --> 00:00:16,290
because it does part of the job.

7
00:00:16,333 --> 00:00:20,170
The first three are quick to spot,
and you'll see each one in action.

8
00:00:20,190 --> 00:00:23,242
The fourth is an answer that
hits half of a two-part goal.

9
00:00:23,262 --> 00:00:25,526
That's question six, the hardest one.

10
00:00:25,546 --> 00:00:28,384
Stay for it, because both
halves have to be there.

11
00:00:28,404 --> 00:00:32,082
One habit fixes all four:
read the goal first,

12
00:00:32,118 --> 00:00:35,241
then find the choice
that does exactly that.

13
00:00:35,261 --> 00:00:38,226
Six SAT-style questions, each
one harder than the last.

14
00:00:38,618 --> 00:00:41,404
Keep score as you go, and
see if you can get all six.

15
00:00:41,447 --> 00:00:44,106
These questions typically
open with the same line.

16
00:00:44,126 --> 00:00:47,340
While researching a topic, a student
has taken the following notes.

17
00:00:47,840 --> 00:00:49,748
Then comes a short list of bullet points,

18
00:00:49,768 --> 00:00:51,305
each one a fact.

19
00:00:51,391 --> 00:00:53,426
After the notes comes the question.

20
00:00:53,676 --> 00:00:56,212
The student wants to, and then a goal.

21
00:00:56,998 --> 00:00:58,818
Emphasize a similarity.

22
00:00:59,212 --> 00:01:01,426
Introduce a person to a new audience.

23
00:01:01,605 --> 00:01:03,522
Present a study and its findings.

24
00:01:03,542 --> 00:01:07,772
Then the same ending every time: which
choice most effectively uses relevant

25
00:01:07,792 --> 00:01:10,685
information from the notes
to accomplish this goal?

26
00:01:10,717 --> 00:01:12,110
Notice what's being tested.

27
00:01:12,539 --> 00:01:14,539
The notes are a pile of facts.

28
00:01:14,932 --> 00:01:18,019
The four choices are usually
built from those same facts,

29
00:01:18,039 --> 00:01:19,717
so they're usually all true.

30
00:01:20,432 --> 00:01:23,662
The SAT isn't asking
which sentence is true.

31
00:01:23,682 --> 00:01:26,003
It's asking which sentence does the job.

32
00:01:26,057 --> 00:01:27,057
Here's the method.

33
00:01:27,307 --> 00:01:31,236
Step one: skip down and read
the goal before the notes.

34
00:01:31,664 --> 00:01:34,414
Underline the key words, like similarity,

35
00:01:34,592 --> 00:01:38,056
difference, unfamiliar
audience, or findings.

36
00:01:38,121 --> 00:01:41,299
Step two: turn the goal into a checklist.

37
00:01:41,835 --> 00:01:45,743
Emphasize a similarity means the
answer has to mention both things,

38
00:01:45,763 --> 00:01:47,727
and show what they share.

39
00:01:48,050 --> 00:01:49,693
One thing alone isn't enough.

40
00:01:49,760 --> 00:01:52,902
Step three: test each choice
against that checklist.

41
00:01:53,368 --> 00:01:54,490
Not, is it true.

42
00:01:54,510 --> 00:01:55,598
Not, is it interesting.

43
00:01:55,618 --> 00:01:56,760
Does it do the goal?

44
00:01:57,331 --> 00:02:01,598
Step four: look back at the notes
only if you need to check a detail.

45
00:02:01,618 --> 00:02:03,081
Most of the time, you won't.

46
00:02:03,152 --> 00:02:04,152
Let's try it.

47
00:02:04,974 --> 00:02:06,116
Goal first.

48
00:02:06,224 --> 00:02:09,632
The student wants to emphasize a
similarity between bats and dolphins.

49
00:02:09,652 --> 00:02:11,723
Key word: similarity.

50
00:02:11,795 --> 00:02:15,437
So the answer has to mention both
animals and something they share.

51
00:02:15,497 --> 00:02:16,497
Now the notes.

52
00:02:17,246 --> 00:02:20,068
Bats are the only mammals
capable of true flight.

53
00:02:20,425 --> 00:02:23,675
Many bats hunt insects in
the dark using echolocation:

54
00:02:23,854 --> 00:02:26,211
they make sounds and
listen for the echoes.

55
00:02:26,710 --> 00:02:29,854
Dolphins are marine mammals
that hunt fish and squid.

56
00:02:30,354 --> 00:02:33,354
Dolphins also locate
prey using echolocation.

57
00:02:33,390 --> 00:02:37,247
Bats make high-pitched calls, while
dolphins produce rapid clicks.

58
00:02:37,345 --> 00:02:38,417
Pause and try it.

59
00:02:42,689 --> 00:02:46,669
A says bats can truly fly, while
dolphins are marine mammals.

60
00:02:46,689 --> 00:02:47,689
That's a difference.

61
00:02:48,368 --> 00:02:49,368
Out.

62
00:02:49,582 --> 00:02:51,368
B is only about dolphins.

63
00:02:51,511 --> 00:02:53,832
A similarity needs both animals.

64
00:02:54,581 --> 00:02:55,341
Out.

65
00:02:55,361 --> 00:03:00,075
D says bats make high-pitched calls,
whereas dolphins produce rapid clicks.

66
00:03:00,718 --> 00:03:02,234
Whereas is a contrast word.

67
00:03:02,254 --> 00:03:03,389
That's a difference too.

68
00:03:03,409 --> 00:03:07,158
C says both bats and dolphins
use echolocation to locate prey.

69
00:03:07,623 --> 00:03:09,675
Both animals, one shared trait.

70
00:03:09,695 --> 00:03:11,229
The answer is C.

71
00:03:11,373 --> 00:03:13,996
And notice: every choice was true.

72
00:03:14,016 --> 00:03:15,959
Accuracy didn't eliminate anything.

73
00:03:15,979 --> 00:03:17,088
The goal did.

74
00:03:17,140 --> 00:03:20,569
Before the next questions, here
are goals that show up often.

75
00:03:21,462 --> 00:03:23,712
Compare or contrast two things.

76
00:03:24,176 --> 00:03:26,069
Emphasize a similarity.

77
00:03:26,354 --> 00:03:30,175
Introduce someone or something to an
audience that's unfamiliar with it.

78
00:03:30,211 --> 00:03:33,997
Present a study, its
method, or its findings.

79
00:03:34,533 --> 00:03:38,130
Explain an advantage,
a cause, or a purpose.

80
00:03:38,150 --> 00:03:40,007
Each goal has its own checklist.

81
00:03:40,578 --> 00:03:43,114
Contrast needs both
things and a difference.

82
00:03:43,328 --> 00:03:47,150
An unfamiliar audience needs an
explanation of who or what something is.

83
00:03:47,506 --> 00:03:50,273
Findings means what the
researchers discovered,

84
00:03:50,293 --> 00:03:51,721
not just what they did.

85
00:03:51,779 --> 00:03:52,779
Question two.

86
00:03:53,814 --> 00:03:55,064
Goal first.

87
00:03:55,422 --> 00:03:59,422
The student wants to emphasize a difference
in where the two telescopes operate.

88
00:03:59,492 --> 00:04:02,885
Two key words here: difference, and where.

89
00:04:02,972 --> 00:04:03,972
The notes.

90
00:04:05,686 --> 00:04:09,094
The Hubble Space Telescope
launched in nineteen ninety.

91
00:04:09,114 --> 00:04:12,221
It orbits Earth a few hundred
miles above the surface.

92
00:04:12,686 --> 00:04:16,006
The James Webb Space Telescope
launched in twenty twenty-one.

93
00:04:16,792 --> 00:04:20,329
It orbits the Sun about one
million miles from Earth.

94
00:04:20,373 --> 00:04:23,818
Both telescopes were built through
international partnerships that include NASA

95
00:04:23,838 --> 00:04:25,552
and the European Space Agency.

96
00:04:25,625 --> 00:04:26,696
Pause and try it.

97
00:04:30,968 --> 00:04:32,540
A is a similarity.

98
00:04:33,147 --> 00:04:34,147
Out.

99
00:04:34,325 --> 00:04:36,290
C only describes Webb.

100
00:04:36,397 --> 00:04:38,519
You can't show a difference
with one telescope.

101
00:04:38,539 --> 00:04:39,311
Out.

102
00:04:39,331 --> 00:04:40,883
B is the trap.

103
00:04:40,903 --> 00:04:45,510
It is a difference: Hubble launched
more than thirty years before Webb.

104
00:04:45,653 --> 00:04:47,632
But the goal said a difference in where,

105
00:04:47,652 --> 00:04:48,652
not when.

106
00:04:49,403 --> 00:04:51,688
Right type of answer, wrong detail.

107
00:04:51,724 --> 00:04:55,060
D puts Hubble orbiting Earth
next to Webb orbiting the Sun,

108
00:04:55,080 --> 00:04:56,740
a million miles away.

109
00:04:56,760 --> 00:04:59,330
A difference, and it's about location.

110
00:04:59,545 --> 00:05:01,330
The answer is D.

111
00:05:01,369 --> 00:05:02,726
Question three.

112
00:05:02,905 --> 00:05:06,905
The student wants to introduce Zaha
Hadid to an audience unfamiliar with her.

113
00:05:07,333 --> 00:05:10,476
That phrase, unfamiliar
audience, is a signal.

114
00:05:10,797 --> 00:05:14,332
The reader has never heard of her, so
the sentence has to tell them who she is.

115
00:05:14,361 --> 00:05:15,361
The notes.

116
00:05:16,325 --> 00:05:19,877
Zaha Hadid, who lived from
nineteen fifty to twenty sixteen,

117
00:05:19,897 --> 00:05:22,110
was an Iraqi-born British architect.

118
00:05:22,289 --> 00:05:24,055
She was known for buildings with flowing,

119
00:05:24,075 --> 00:05:25,361
curved forms.

120
00:05:25,431 --> 00:05:28,341
In two thousand four, she became
the first woman to win the Pritzker

121
00:05:28,361 --> 00:05:31,430
Architecture Prize, one of
architecture's highest honors.

122
00:05:31,450 --> 00:05:35,200
She designed the London Aquatics Centre
for the twenty twelve Olympic Games.

123
00:05:35,248 --> 00:05:36,319
Pause and try it.

124
00:05:40,592 --> 00:05:43,235
A says, Hadid designed the
London Aquatics Centre.

125
00:05:43,627 --> 00:05:45,465
If you've never heard of her, you'd ask:

126
00:05:45,485 --> 00:05:45,822
who's Hadid?

127
00:05:45,842 --> 00:05:48,071
D has the same problem.

128
00:05:48,091 --> 00:05:50,985
It talks about Hadid's buildings
but never says who she is.

129
00:05:51,065 --> 00:05:54,044
B is about the prize going to
a woman for the first time,

130
00:05:54,064 --> 00:05:55,529
but it never names her.

131
00:05:55,850 --> 00:05:58,065
You can't introduce someone
without naming them.

132
00:05:58,144 --> 00:06:01,946
C: Zaha Hadid, an Iraqi-born
British architect known for flowing,

133
00:06:01,966 --> 00:06:06,680
curved buildings, was the first woman
to win the Pritzker Architecture Prize.

134
00:06:06,966 --> 00:06:10,466
Full name, who she is,
and why she matters.

135
00:06:10,858 --> 00:06:12,716
The answer is C.

136
00:06:12,772 --> 00:06:13,807
Quick tip.

137
00:06:13,879 --> 00:06:18,073
For an unfamiliar audience, look for
a description right next to the name,

138
00:06:18,093 --> 00:06:19,807
usually set off by commas.

139
00:06:20,307 --> 00:06:22,644
If the goal said the
audience already knew her,

140
00:06:22,664 --> 00:06:24,379
that description wouldn't be needed.

141
00:06:24,451 --> 00:06:25,522
Question four.

142
00:06:26,022 --> 00:06:29,129
The student wants to present
the study and its findings.

143
00:06:29,415 --> 00:06:30,415
Two parts.

144
00:06:30,594 --> 00:06:32,878
The study means who did what.

145
00:06:33,593 --> 00:06:36,022
The findings mean what they discovered.

146
00:06:36,086 --> 00:06:37,086
The notes.

147
00:06:38,585 --> 00:06:41,994
Arctic terns are seabirds that breed
in the far north and spend the northern

148
00:06:42,014 --> 00:06:43,657
winter in Antarctic waters.

149
00:06:43,979 --> 00:06:46,601
A research team attached
tiny tracking devices,

150
00:06:46,621 --> 00:06:49,049
each weighing about one point four grams,

151
00:06:49,121 --> 00:06:51,834
to Arctic terns nesting
in Greenland and Iceland.

152
00:06:51,854 --> 00:06:55,568
The researchers used the devices to
follow the birds' yearly migration.

153
00:06:56,389 --> 00:06:59,548
They found that the terns traveled an
average of about seventy thousand nine

154
00:06:59,568 --> 00:07:01,103
hundred kilometers in a year.

155
00:07:01,135 --> 00:07:02,206
Pause and try it.

156
00:07:06,479 --> 00:07:08,316
B is background about the bird.

157
00:07:08,336 --> 00:07:10,156
No study, no findings.

158
00:07:10,407 --> 00:07:13,101
C describes what the researchers did,

159
00:07:13,121 --> 00:07:15,836
the tracking devices,
but not what they found.

160
00:07:16,371 --> 00:07:17,585
That's half the goal.

161
00:07:17,644 --> 00:07:20,216
D gives the finding, but drops the study.

162
00:07:20,716 --> 00:07:23,053
It reads like a general fact about terns,

163
00:07:23,073 --> 00:07:24,573
not a research result.

164
00:07:24,604 --> 00:07:25,676
A has both.

165
00:07:26,069 --> 00:07:30,176
Researchers used tracking devices
on terns from Greenland and Iceland,

166
00:07:30,211 --> 00:07:34,120
and found the birds traveled about
seventy thousand nine hundred kilometers a

167
00:07:34,140 --> 00:07:35,140
year.

168
00:07:35,426 --> 00:07:37,604
The answer is A.

169
00:07:37,672 --> 00:07:39,851
Let's name the traps you've seen so far.

170
00:07:39,922 --> 00:07:42,708
Trap one: true, but off goal.

171
00:07:44,244 --> 00:07:45,294
Every fact checks out.

172
00:07:45,314 --> 00:07:46,815
It just does a different job.

173
00:07:46,884 --> 00:07:50,400
Trap two: right type, wrong detail.

174
00:07:50,420 --> 00:07:53,670
A difference, but in
when instead of where.

175
00:07:53,813 --> 00:07:55,526
Trap three: half the goal.

176
00:07:55,777 --> 00:07:58,955
The study without the findings,
or the findings without the study.

177
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:01,571
Trap four: one side only.

178
00:08:02,142 --> 00:08:05,285
If the goal involves two things,
the answer has to mention both.

179
00:08:05,392 --> 00:08:06,678
Question five.

180
00:08:07,035 --> 00:08:11,249
The student wants to explain an advantage
of LED bulbs over incandescent bulbs.

181
00:08:11,714 --> 00:08:16,106
Checklist: it has to be about LEDs, and
it has to say what makes them better.

182
00:08:16,191 --> 00:08:17,191
The notes.

183
00:08:17,656 --> 00:08:22,227
Incandescent bulbs make light by heating
a thin wire filament until it glows.

184
00:08:22,656 --> 00:08:26,135
About ninety percent of the energy an
incandescent bulb uses is given off as

185
00:08:26,155 --> 00:08:27,155
heat.

186
00:08:27,405 --> 00:08:31,564
LED bulbs make light using tiny electronic
components called light-emitting

187
00:08:31,584 --> 00:08:32,359
diodes.

188
00:08:32,379 --> 00:08:33,252
According to the U.S.

189
00:08:33,272 --> 00:08:37,145
Department of Energy, residential LED
bulbs use at least seventy-five percent

190
00:08:37,165 --> 00:08:41,950
less energy than incandescent bulbs and
can last up to twenty-five times longer.

191
00:08:42,007 --> 00:08:43,078
Pause and try it.

192
00:08:46,351 --> 00:08:48,886
A and B explain how each bulb works.

193
00:08:48,994 --> 00:08:51,866
True, but how something
works isn't an advantage.

194
00:08:51,886 --> 00:08:53,687
C is closer.

195
00:08:53,707 --> 00:08:56,136
It's a weakness of incandescent bulbs.

196
00:08:57,136 --> 00:09:01,565
But it never mentions LEDs, so it
can't explain an advantage of LEDs.

197
00:09:01,624 --> 00:09:05,604
D says LED bulbs use at least seventy-five
percent less energy and can last up

198
00:09:05,624 --> 00:09:07,445
to twenty-five times longer.

199
00:09:07,910 --> 00:09:10,588
That's an advantage,
stated directly about LEDs.

200
00:09:10,803 --> 00:09:14,025
The answer is D.

201
00:09:14,045 --> 00:09:16,830
Last one, and it's harder.

202
00:09:17,544 --> 00:09:23,167
The student wants to emphasize that the
spacecraft that launched first was not

203
00:09:23,187 --> 00:09:25,331
the first to reach interstellar space.

204
00:09:25,374 --> 00:09:26,374
The notes.

205
00:09:26,910 --> 00:09:30,176
In nineteen seventy-seven,
NASA launched two spacecraft,

206
00:09:30,196 --> 00:09:32,374
Voyager one and Voyager two.

207
00:09:32,731 --> 00:09:35,410
Voyager two launched on August twentieth.

208
00:09:35,552 --> 00:09:37,819
Voyager one launched sixteen days later,

209
00:09:37,839 --> 00:09:39,196
on September fifth.

210
00:09:39,732 --> 00:09:42,649
Voyager one reached interstellar
space in twenty twelve.

211
00:09:42,669 --> 00:09:45,649
Voyager two reached interstellar
space in twenty eighteen.

212
00:09:45,669 --> 00:09:49,276
Voyager two is the only spacecraft
to have visited Uranus and Neptune.

213
00:09:49,329 --> 00:09:50,472
Pause and try it.

214
00:09:51,115 --> 00:09:52,758
Take a little longer on this one.

215
00:09:56,973 --> 00:09:58,330
Break the goal down.

216
00:09:58,759 --> 00:10:02,203
To show that the first to launch
wasn't the first to arrive,

217
00:10:02,223 --> 00:10:05,810
the sentence needs two things:
which one launched first,

218
00:10:05,830 --> 00:10:07,366
and which one arrived first.

219
00:10:07,446 --> 00:10:09,625
D is about Uranus and Neptune.

220
00:10:10,445 --> 00:10:12,768
Interesting, but off goal.

221
00:10:12,839 --> 00:10:14,997
C gives the launch order only.

222
00:10:15,017 --> 00:10:19,141
A gives the arrival order only: Voyager
one got there six years before Voyager

223
00:10:19,161 --> 00:10:19,604
two.

224
00:10:19,624 --> 00:10:22,589
A is the tempting one, because
it sounds like the main point.

225
00:10:22,629 --> 00:10:25,824
But A never says Voyager
two launched first,

226
00:10:25,844 --> 00:10:27,665
so there's nothing surprising about it.

227
00:10:27,808 --> 00:10:29,951
Only B has both halves.

228
00:10:30,522 --> 00:10:34,538
Although Voyager two launched
sixteen days before Voyager one,

229
00:10:34,558 --> 00:10:38,058
it reached interstellar space
six years after its twin.

230
00:10:38,594 --> 00:10:40,444
The answer is B.

231
00:10:40,464 --> 00:10:42,642
Here's the whole method on one card.

232
00:10:43,178 --> 00:10:47,464
One: read the goal first
and underline the key words.

233
00:10:47,928 --> 00:10:50,141
Two: turn it into a checklist.

234
00:10:50,749 --> 00:10:52,428
What has to be in the answer?

235
00:10:52,678 --> 00:10:53,749
Both things?

236
00:10:53,785 --> 00:10:54,892
A description?

237
00:10:54,999 --> 00:10:56,178
The findings?

238
00:10:56,230 --> 00:10:59,102
Three: test each choice
against the checklist,

239
00:10:59,122 --> 00:11:00,551
not against the notes.

240
00:11:01,301 --> 00:11:04,729
Four: check the notes
only to settle a detail.

241
00:11:04,873 --> 00:11:08,300
And if the goal has two parts,
make sure the answer has both.

242
00:11:08,355 --> 00:11:09,462
One pacing note.

243
00:11:09,998 --> 00:11:13,799
You get about seventy-one seconds
per question in Reading and Writing,

244
00:11:13,819 --> 00:11:14,891
on average.

245
00:11:15,568 --> 00:11:17,747
Because you aren't studying every note,

246
00:11:18,069 --> 00:11:20,855
a notes question can
often take less than that,

247
00:11:21,391 --> 00:11:23,813
which buys time for harder questions.

248
00:11:23,833 --> 00:11:24,833
Recap.

249
00:11:26,119 --> 00:11:28,920
On notes questions, the
choices are usually all true,

250
00:11:28,940 --> 00:11:30,369
so the goal decides.

251
00:11:31,189 --> 00:11:34,741
Read it first, make a checklist, and
cross out anything that does a different

252
00:11:34,761 --> 00:11:36,189
job, even if it's accurate.

253
00:11:36,278 --> 00:11:40,079
For more practice, the College Board's
free Question Bank lets you filter for

254
00:11:40,099 --> 00:11:42,742
rhetorical synthesis
under Expression of Ideas.

